 What is /init?

  The /init command is a built-in Claude Code command that creates your project's memory system. When you run it:

  1. Creates CLAUDE.md file - This is your project's "memory" file where Claude stores:
    - Project overview and purpose
    - Architecture and structure
    - Build commands
    - Coding standards
    - Common workflows
    - Development guidelines
  2. Location: Creates the file at either:
    - .claude/CLAUDE.md (project-specific, recommended)
    - ./CLAUDE.md (root of project)
  3. What happens: Claude analyzes your project and generates initial documentation automatically.

  What is the .claude/ folder?

  The .claude/ folder is the Claude Code configuration directory for your project. It's similar to .git/ or .vscode/ folders.

  Structure:

  .claude/
  ├── CLAUDE.md              # Project memory (created by /init)
  ├── settings.json          # Hooks configuration (we'll create)
  ├── skills/                # Custom skills (we'll create)
  │   ├── spring-crud/
  │   │   └── SKILL.md
  │   └── test-generator/
  │       └── SKILL.md
  ├── agents/                # Custom agents (we'll create)
  │   ├── java-architect.md
  │   └── code-reviewer.md
  ├── rules/                 # Project standards (we'll create)
  │   ├── java-style.md
  │   └── testing.md
  ├── hooks/                 # Automation scripts (we'll create)
  │   ├── auto-format.py
  │   └── check-tests.sh
  └── mcp-servers/           # Custom MCP servers (optional)

  How is it created?

  1. Automatic - /init creates .claude/CLAUDE.md
  2. Manual - You create the folder: mkdir -p .claude/skills
  3. By us - We'll create the subdirectories (skills/, agents/, rules/, etc.)

  Why is it useful?

  For Claude Code:
  - Remembers context - CLAUDE.md is automatically loaded in every conversation
  - Enforces standards - Rules in .claude/rules/ are automatically applied
  - Enables automation - Skills, agents, and hooks extend Claude's capabilities
  - Team collaboration - Commit .claude/ to git so the whole team benefits

  For you:
  - Consistency - Everyone (human or Claude) follows same patterns
  - Productivity - Custom skills automate repetitive tasks
  - Quality - Hooks enforce quality gates automatically
  - Knowledge sharing - CLAUDE.md documents your project for new team members

  Example: CLAUDE.md in action

  When CLAUDE.md exists and contains:
  ## Build Commands
  ./gradlew build
  ./gradlew bootRun

  ## Coding Standards
  - Use constructor injection, not @Autowired
  - Service classes must be final

  Then every time you start a conversation with Claude Code:
  - Claude knows how to build your project
  - Claude follows your coding standards automatically
  - You don't have to repeat yourself

  Should we commit .claude/ to git?

  YES! (Mostly)

  ✅ Commit these:
  - .claude/CLAUDE.md - Project memory
  - .claude/skills/ - Custom skills (team benefit)
  - .claude/agents/ - Custom agents (team benefit)
  - .claude/rules/ - Project standards (team benefit)
  - .mcp.json - MCP configuration (team benefit)

  ❌ Don't commit (add to .gitignore):
  - .claude/settings.local.json - Personal hooks/settings
  - Any files with secrets/credentials

  Example workflow:

  # Day 1: You create a skill
  mkdir -p .claude/skills/spring-crud
  # Create SKILL.md

  # Commit to git
  git add .claude/
  git commit -m "Add spring-crud skill"
  git push

  # Day 2: Your teammate pulls
  git pull

  # Now your teammate can use the skill too!
  > "Use spring-crud to create a User entity"
